\f3\fs18 \cf0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}
\f0\b\fs26 Mathematical structure
\f1\b0 : Consider frames related by velocity (v) along (x) (units (c=1)). Lorentz transformations (homogeneous):\uc0\u8232 [ x\'92 = \\gamma (x - \\beta t), \\quad y\'92 = y, \\quad z\'92 = z, \\quad t\'92 = \\gamma (t - \\beta x), ]\u8232 where (\\beta = v), (\\gamma = 1/\\sqrt\{1-\\beta^2\}), plus spatial rotations. Poincar\'e9 generalized to arbitrary boosts/rotations and proved closure: composing two yields another (with adjusted parameters). He showed associativity, identity ((\\beta=0)), and inverses ((-\\beta)) hold, satisfying group axioms. He decomposed any element as a boost followed by rotation, preserving the quadratic invariant:\u8232 [ x^2 + y^2 + z^2 - t^2 = \\text\{constant\} ]\u8232 (Minkowski metric precursor; he used imaginary time (t \\to it) for 4D rotation analogy). This is the Lorentz group\'92s defining property: it is the isometry group of Minkowski spacetime, (O(1,3)). He introduced four-vectors (e.g., ((x,y,z,t)), current (( \\rho, \\mathbf\{j\} ))) and showed Maxwell\'92s equations are covariant.016\
