\f0\b \cf0 Casimir operators
\f1\b0  (often shortened to \'93Casimir\'94 in physics contexts) are a cornerstone of Lie-group and Lie-algebra representation theory, and your question directly follows from the Poincar\'e9-group discussion. In our conversation on Poincar\'e9\'92s group-theoretic contributions, Lorentz Ether Theory, motion-versus-geometry, and relativistic symmetries, these operators are precisely the invariants that label the irreducible representations (irreps) of the Poincar\'e9 group\'97the very symmetry group of Minkowski spacetime that classifies elementary particles in quantum field theory (QFT). They encode intrinsic properties (mass and spin/helicity) that remain unchanged under all allowed \'93motions\'94 (Lorentz boosts, rotations, translations). I\'92ll first clarify what a Casimir operator 
\f2\i is
